# APPLETS THIS KERNEL CANNOT SUPPORT.
#
# busybox is one binary under ~400 names, and the names are chosen at
# BUSYBOX config time while what they need is decided at KERNEL config
# time. Nothing connects the two, so the base image shipped commands
# that could not work: `ipcs` answered "kernel not configured for
# message queues" and `ipcrm` answered "unknown errror in id (1)"
# (busybox's typo) for the life of the project, because
# CONFIG_SYSVIPC is n. A command that cannot work is worse than one
# that is absent -- RFC 0026's argument for deleting `idle3`, RFC
# 0040's for `bashbug`, and RFC 0040 roadmap 2's for busybox's own
# `man`, which is the same defect one layer up.
#
# Format:  <applet>  <CONFIG_SYMBOL>  <one line: what it needs the symbol for>
#
# The DECISION is derived, not written here: scripts/prune-dead-applets.sh
# reads the GENERATED kernel config and removes an applet's symlink only
# when its symbol is absent. So turning a symbol on restores the command
# on the next build with no edit to this file -- which is the whole
# reason it is a table of dependencies rather than a list of removals.
#
# NEVER answer this question from kernel/config-x86_64. That file is a
# ~280-option SUBSET, so a symbol it does not mention is not thereby
# off: CONFIG_SWAP and CONFIG_HOTPLUG_CPU are both unmentioned there
# and both `y` in the generated config, and swap really works on a
# booted machine. Three of this table's first four entries were wrong
# for exactly that reason before the generated config was consulted.
#
# The APPLET stays in the binary either way; only its claim on a name
# in PATH goes, exactly as with /sbin/init. `busybox ipcs` still runs
# for anyone who wants to watch it fail.
#
# Found while measuring the util-linux gap (RFC 0040 roadmap 3,
# tests/utillinux-gap/) -- the question "what can this machine actually
# run" has two halves, and this is the half nobody had asked.
#
# A NAME THIS BUSYBOX DOES NOT BUILD IS DEAD WEIGHT IN A TABLE ABOUT
# DEAD WEIGHT, and the test checks that against the shipped binary.
# Its first run caught four: `flashcp`, `flash_eraseall`, `flash_lock`
# and `flash_unlock` are MTD tools this busybox config does not
# compile at all, so the rows would have sat here forever doing
# nothing. They are out; if the busybox config ever turns them on,
# CONFIG_MTD is what they would need.

ipcs            CONFIG_SYSVIPC          System V message queues, semaphores and shared memory
ipcrm           CONFIG_SYSVIPC          removes the System V objects ipcs lists
hwclock         CONFIG_RTC_CLASS        /dev/rtc0, which only the RTC class driver creates
rtcwake         CONFIG_RTC_CLASS        an RTC alarm as a wake source for suspend
nbd-client      CONFIG_BLK_DEV_NBD      the network block device
ubiattach       CONFIG_MTD_UBI          UBI volumes on raw flash
ubidetach       CONFIG_MTD_UBI          UBI volumes on raw flash
ubimkvol        CONFIG_MTD_UBI          UBI volumes on raw flash
ubirmvol        CONFIG_MTD_UBI          UBI volumes on raw flash
ubirename       CONFIG_MTD_UBI          UBI volumes on raw flash
ubiupdatevol    CONFIG_MTD_UBI          UBI volumes on raw flash
vconfig         CONFIG_VLAN_8021Q       802.1q VLAN interfaces
